 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "«الأعراب» are the desert dwellers of that time; 97:2-4 carries As-Sa'di's qualified comparison («ففي البادية أشد وأغلظ مما في الحاضرة») and 99 opens with his «وليس الأعراب كلهم مذمومين», so no people is blamed as a whole. No people drawn. Coins for the spending, light for «ألا إنها قربة لهم». No motif on «صلوات الرسول». STAGING v4: c1 (97-98): a desert camp by day (both tafsirs: the Bedouins are the people of the desert). Two groups of three men sit by the tents; no gesture and no close shot in 97, which speaks of the desert people in general (the gloss keeps As-Sa'di's qualified comparison): wide shots and gentle pans only; a book plate on 97:8-9 (the limits of what was revealed, gone before the Name and the Messenger); the shot is wide while they are recited. 98 is about some of them («ومن الأعراب من»): only the right group acts. A coins plate on 98:6 (what they spend) that undraws on 98:8 (counted as a loss); they shake their heads on 98:7 (As-Sa'di: they hardly pay it except unwillingly); an hourglass on 98:8-10 (they wait for turns of misfortune on you); the camera holds on them for 98:11-13 (the evil turn is upon them); nothing is drawn for any misfortune. c2 (99): the same desert, three believers (the believing Bedouins); a coins plate on their spending (99:9-10); nobody gives (their spending was brought to the Messenger, who is never shown, and no recipient is invented); the shot is wide while the Messenger's prayers are recited (99:13-15); a light plate on 99:16 (a nearness for them) and a soft sparkle on them on 99:18."
 },
